course: dictionary
lesson: 8048
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The word regular can mean happening again and again, or usual, or something in maths or grammar. The context tells you which meaning the writer wants. «Gardens need regular weeding, mowing and watering or they quickly grow wild.» Here regular means done again and again at set times.
explain: «My regular seat is by the window.» Regular means usual, the seat you always sit in. To check, swap in another word such as usual. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
explain: The subject tells you the meaning too. In maths, regular describes a shape. In grammar, regular describes a verb. In a café, a regular is a person who often goes there.
fact: Regular has several meanings, so always check the context.
fact: «Regular» can mean happening again and again at the same times, like «regular meals» or «a regular heartbeat».
fact: «Regular» can also mean usual or normal, like «my regular seat».
fact: In maths, a regular shape has all sides the same length and all angles the same size. A square is regular.
fact: A regular verb makes its past with -ed, like walk and walked. Irregular verbs, like go and went, do not.
exercise: «Zara needs regular checks at the optician.» What does regular mean here? | Very large | *Happening again and again at set times | Ending in -ed | Ruled with straight lines
exercise: Which of these is a regular verb? | run–ran | *walk–walked | go–went | eat–ate
exercise: A regular shape has all its sides and angles equal. Which shape is regular? | A rectangle | A trapezium | *A square | A kite
test: «Pocket money is regular money parents give children.» What does regular mean here? | Given once, as a surprise | *Given again and again at set times | Hidden under the bed | Given only to strangers
test: Ali is a regular at the café. What is a regular? | A verb in the past tense | A shape with four sides | *A person who often goes to the same place | A cake sold at the café
exam: In the sentence «My regular seat is by the window», regular means what? | Happening every hour on the clock | Past tense of a verb | *Usual, the seat you always sit in | Having all sides equal
